## (2) that one failure is not this change — A/B on the same export

  The failing assertion is on `.minivic-dock`'s opacity, which this change does
  not touch. Proven by stripping the new rule out of the built stylesheet and
  running the same test against the same export:

  $ perl -0pi -e 's/\@media \(max-width:87\.4375rem\)\{\.minivic-launcher\{...\}\}//' \
      out/_next/static/css/213772871f03baae.css        # rule count → 0
  $ npx playwright test tests/monochrome/minivic-launcher.spec.ts -g "MONO-MV-02 @ 390"
      1 passed (3.3s)

  $ cp /tmp/mv5-css-with-fix.css out/_next/static/css/213772871f03baae.css   # rule count → 1
  $ npx playwright test tests/monochrome/minivic-launcher.spec.ts -g "MONO-MV-02 @ 390"
      1 passed (3.3s)                                   ← the fix is in, and it passes

  So the difference was not the rule. The 8-second `expect.poll` on the dock's
  IntersectionObserver is what timed out, in a 1.5-minute four-file run on a
  4-core VPS with three other lanes building. It is a timing flake, and it is
  the same assertion W1-MV3 already recorded red on b9f5195 for a different
  reason.

